            WHY USE A LOAD BALANCER? 

                - A load balancer does much more than simply distribute traffic. It plays a key role in improving the scalability, availability, and reliability of your application.
            
                - The primary purpose of a load balancer is to spread incoming traffic across multiple EC2 instances. By sharing the workload, it prevents any single instance from becoming overloaded and helps deliver consistent performance to users.

                - A load balancer also provides a single point of access for your application. Instead of connecting directly to individual EC2 instances, users connect to the load balancer using its DNS name. The load balancer then determines which backend instance should handle each request, hiding the complexity of your infrastructure.

                - Another important feature is automatic failover. If an EC2 instance becomes unhealthy or stops responding, the load balancer automatically stops sending traffic to it and redirects requests to the remaining healthy instances. This helps keep your application available without requiring manual intervention.

