                - Load balancers can also handle SSL/TLS termination. Instead of each EC2 instance managing HTTPS encryption and decryption, the load balancer processes secure connections on behalf of your application. This simplifies certificate management and allows your instances to focus on serving application requests.

                - In some applications, users need to remain connected to the same backend server throughout their session. This feature, known as sticky sessions or session persistence, uses cookies to route a user's requests to the same EC2 instance when required.

                - Load balancers are also designed for high availability. They can distribute traffic across EC2 instances running in multiple Availability Zones. If one Availability Zone experiences an outage, traffic is automatically routed to healthy instances in the remaining zones, helping your application stay online.

                - Finally, load balancers can separate public and private traffic. For example, one load balancer can receive requests from internet users, while another handles communication between internal services. This improves both security and traffic management in larger architectures.

                - Overall, a load balancer is an essential component of modern cloud applications. It distributes traffic, monitors instance health, handles failover, supports secure HTTPS connections, and improves both scalability and high availability.



