        TYPES OF LOAD BALANCERS 
            - AWS offers several types of load balancers, each designed for different workloads and networking requirements. While they all distribute incoming traffic across multiple resources to improve performance and availability, they operate at different layers of the network and provide different features depending on the application.
            
            - The Classic Load Balancer (CLB), introduced in 2009, is the original generation of AWS load balancer. It supports HTTP, HTTPS, TCP, and SSL traffic and is suitable for basic applications. However, it lacks many of the advanced routing capabilities and performance improvements found in newer load balancers, so AWS generally recommends using the newer options for modern deployments.
            
            - The Application Load Balancer (ALB), released in 2016, operates at Layer 7 (the Application Layer) and is designed for HTTP, HTTPS, and WebSocket traffic. It can make intelligent routing decisions based on request content, such as URLs, headers, query parameters, and cookies, making it ideal for modern web applications, APIs, microservices, and containerised applications.
        
            - The Network Load Balancer (NLB), introduced in 2017, operates at Layer 4 (the Transport Layer) and is built for high-performance, low-latency TCP, UDP, and TLS traffic. It is capable of handling millions of requests per second, making it well suited for applications that require high throughput and minimal latency, such as real-time gaming, financial trading platforms, and other performance-critical services.
            
            - The Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB) operates at Layer 3 (the Network Layer) and uses the IP protocol. Rather than balancing web traffic, it is designed to deploy, scale, and manage third-party virtual network appliances, such as firewalls, intrusion detection systems (IDS), and traffic monitoring tools, making it the preferred choice for advanced networking and security architectures.

            - In most cases, ALB is the best choice for modern web applications, NLB is ideal for high-performance network traffic, and GWLB is used for security and networking appliances. Although the Classic Load Balancer is still available, AWS recommends using the newer generation of load balancers because they provide greater scalability, flexibility, and more advanced routing features.
            
        LOAD BALANMCER SECURITY GROUP

            - Security groups act as virtual firewalls that control the inbound and outbound traffic to AWS resources. When using an Application Load Balancer (ALB) with EC2 instances, it is common practice to use a two-tier security group configuration. This ensures that traffic is routed securely while protecting your backend servers from direct access.

            - The first security group is attached to the load balancer. Its purpose is to allow users to connect to your application from the internet. Typically, it allows HTTP (port 80) and HTTPS (port 443) traffic from 0.0.0.0/0, which means it accepts requests from any IP address on the internet. The load balancer acts as the application's public entry point, receiving all incoming traffic before deciding where it should be sent.
            
            - The second security group is attached to the EC2 instances behind the load balancer. Unlike the load balancer, these instances are not exposed directly to the internet. Instead of allowing traffic from any IP address, their security group only allows inbound traffic from the load balancer's security group. This means the EC2 instances will only accept requests that have already passed through the load balancer, preventing users from bypassing it and connecting directly to the servers.
            
            - This two-tier security group setup creates a secure flow of traffic. Users send requests to the load balancer, the load balancer verifies the traffic against its security group rules, and then forwards legitimate requests to one of the healthy EC2 instances. Because the EC2 instances only trust traffic coming from the load balancer, they remain protected from direct external access while still being able to serve user requests.
    
            - A useful way to think about this is to imagine the load balancer as the front door of a secure office building. Visitors can enter through the front door, but they cannot walk directly into every office. Instead, security directs them to the correct room. Similarly, the load balancer receives all incoming traffic, checks that it is allowed, and forwards it only to the appropriate EC2 instance. The EC2 instances never communicate directly with internet users—they only communicate with the trusted load balancer.
    
            - This architecture is widely used in AWS because it improves both security and scalability. The load balancer provides a single, secure entry point for users, while the EC2 instances remain shielded from direct internet access. At the same time, the load balancer can distribute traffic across multiple instances, helping applications remain highly available, secure, and able to handle increasing workloads.


        WHAT IS A APPLICATION LOAD BALANCER 
            
            - An Application Load Balancer (ALB) is one of AWS's most commonly used load balancers and is designed for modern web applications. It operates at Layer 7 (the Application Layer) of the OSI model, meaning it understands HTTP and HTTPS traffic. Unlike lower-layer load balancers, an ALB can inspect the contents of a request, such as URLs, headers, cookies, query strings, and host names, allowing it to make intelligent routing decisions based on the request itself rather than simply forwarding network traffic.

            - One of the ALB's biggest advantages is its ability to distribute traffic across multiple target groups. These target groups can contain EC2 instances, containers running on Amazon ECS, or AWS Lambda functions, making the ALB highly flexible for different application architectures. It can even route traffic to multiple applications running on the same server, which is especially useful in containerised environments where several services may share a single EC2 instance.
        
            - The ALB supports modern protocols such as HTTP/2 and WebSockets. HTTP/2 improves application performance by allowing multiple requests to be sent over a single connection, reducing latency and improving efficiency. WebSocket support enables persistent, two-way communication between clients and servers, making the ALB ideal for real-time applications such as chat applications, online gaming, live dashboards, and collaboration tools.
            
            - One of the most powerful features of an ALB is its advanced routing capabilities. It supports path-based routing, allowing requests such as /users, /posts, and /comments to be directed to different target groups. It also supports host-based routing, where requests for different domains, such as blog.example.com and shop.example.com, can be routed to separate applications using the same load balancer. Additionally, the ALB supports header-based and query string routing, enabling traffic to be directed based on information such as device type, browser, or URL parameters. These features provide fine-grained control over how requests are handled and are ideal for personalised applications and complex web architectures.
        
            - The ALB is particularly well suited to microservices and container-based applications. In environments such as Docker or Amazon ECS, multiple services often run on different ports of the same server. The ALB supports dynamic port mapping, allowing it to automatically route requests to the correct container regardless of which port it is using. This makes it much easier to deploy, scale, and manage containerised applications.
        
            - Compared to the older Classic Load Balancer (CLB), the ALB is far more efficient and cost-effective. A CLB typically requires a separate load balancer for each application, whereas a single ALB can manage multiple applications using its intelligent routing features. For example, a company running separate Users, Posts, and Comments microservices can use one ALB to route /users requests to one service, /posts to another, and /comments to a third. This reduces infrastructure costs, simplifies management, and makes the ALB the preferred load balancer for modern cloud-native applications.

