            - ALB GOOD TO KNOW

                - One useful feature of an Application Load Balancer (ALB) is that AWS automatically assigns it a DNS hostname in the format name.region.elb.amazonaws.com. This hostname provides a public endpoint that clients use to access your application. If you want users to access your application using a custom domain name, such as www.example.com, you can create a DNS record in Amazon Route 53 (or another DNS provider) that points your domain to the ALB's DNS hostname.


                - Another important concept is that an ALB terminates the client connection. This means the user's connection ends at the load balancer, and the ALB establishes a new connection to the EC2 instance or other target. As a result, your application does not see the client's public IP address directly. Instead, it sees the private IP address of the ALB, which acts as the intermediary between the client and your application.
            
                - To ensure your application can still identify the original client, the ALB automatically adds several HTTP headers to the forwarded request. The most important is X-Forwarded-For, which contains the client's original IP address. This is commonly used for logging, geolocation, rate limiting, security auditing, and detecting suspicious activity. The ALB also adds X-Forwarded-Port, which indicates the port the client used to connect (such as 80 or 443), and X-Forwarded-Proto, which identifies whether the original request used HTTP or HTTPS. These headers are useful for applications that need to redirect users, enforce HTTPS, or record connection details.
            
                - A key point to remember is that if your application needs the client's real IP address, it must be configured to read the X-Forwarded-For header rather than relying on the source IP of the incoming connection. This is a common practice in AWS architectures because all client requests pass through the ALB before reaching the backend servers. Understanding how the ALB handles client connections and forwards this information is important for building secure, scalable, and well-monitored web applications.



NETWORK LOADBALANCING

    - A Network Load Balancer (NLB) is an AWS load balancer designed to distribute Layer 4 (Transport Layer) traffic across multiple targets, such as EC2 instances. Unlike an Application Load Balancer (ALB), which understands HTTP and HTTPS requests, an NLB works with TCP, UDP, and TLS traffic. It makes routing decisions based on network information, such as the source IP address, destination IP address, port number, and protocol, rather than inspecting the contents of the request. This makes it much faster and more efficient for handling large volumes of network traffic.
    
    - An NLB sits between users and your application, accepting incoming network connections and forwarding them to healthy targets. It continuously performs health checks on registered EC2 instances and automatically stops sending traffic to any instance that becomes unhealthy. By distributing connections across multiple healthy instances, the NLB helps improve high availability, fault tolerance, and application reliability, ensuring users experience little or no disruption if a server fails.

    - One of the biggest advantages of an NLB is its high performance. Because it operates at Layer 4 and does not inspect HTTP requests, it can process millions of connections per second with very low latency. It also supports static IP addresses, making it ideal for applications that require fixed IPs, such as systems protected by firewalls or applications that rely on IP whitelisting. This is a key difference from an ALB, which uses a DNS name rather than static IP addresses.

    - Network Load Balancers are commonly used for high-throughput, low-latency applications, including real-time gaming, financial trading platforms, Voice over IP (VoIP), messaging systems, IoT applications, and database connections. They are the preferred choice whenever an application requires fast, efficient handling of TCP, UDP, or TLS traffic without the advanced HTTP routing capabilities provided by an Application Load Balancer. In contrast, if an application needs features such as path-based routing, host-based routing, or routing based on headers and cookies, an Application Load Balancer (ALB) is generally the better option.

        STICKY SESSIONS

            - Sticky sessions, also known as session affinity, are a feature of load balancers that ensure a user's requests are consistently sent to the same backend server for the duration of their session. Under normal circumstances, a load balancer distributes each new request across all healthy servers. However, when sticky sessions are enabled, the load balancer remembers which server handled a user's first request and continues routing all subsequent requests to that same server.

            - Sticky sessions are useful when an application stores session data locally on an individual server. For example, after a user logs into a website, the server may store information such as their login status, shopping cart, or user preferences in its local memory. If the next request is sent to a different server, that server will not have access to the user's session data, which could result in the user being logged out or losing the contents of their shopping cart. By keeping the user connected to the same server, sticky sessions ensure a consistent experience.

            - Application Load Balancers (ALBs) implement sticky sessions using cookies. When a user first connects, the ALB selects a healthy target and returns a cookie to the user's browser. On every subsequent request, the browser sends this cookie back to the ALB, allowing it to identify which server previously handled the user's session and route the request back to that same server. This process continues until the cookie expires or the session ends.

            - If the server handling a user's session becomes unhealthy or fails, the load balancer detects the failure through its health checks and automatically routes the user's requests to another healthy server. However, if the application stores session data only on the original server, the user may lose their session and be required to log in again because the new server does not have access to the previous session information.

            - Sticky sessions are beneficial for applications that rely on locally stored session data, but they also have some disadvantages. Because users remain connected to the same server, traffic may not be distributed evenly across all available instances. This can reduce the effectiveness of load balancing and make it more difficult to scale applications as demand increases.

            - For this reason, modern cloud applications generally avoid relying on sticky sessions. Instead, they store session data in a shared session store, such as Amazon ElastiCache (Redis), Amazon DynamoDB, or a database. This allows every EC2 instance to access the same session information, meaning any healthy server can process a user's request. As a result, traffic is distributed more evenly, applications scale more effectively, and users continue to have a seamless experience even if individual servers are added, removed, or replaced.


